@@@ problem 35 search-insert-position
title: Search Insert Position
difficulty: Easy
topics: Array, Binary Search
params: nums, target
time: O(log n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=logn space=1

=== description
Given a sorted array of **distinct** integers `nums` and a `target`, return the index of `target` if it is present. Otherwise return the index where it would be inserted to keep the array sorted.

Your algorithm must run in **O(log n)** time.

=== constraints
- `1 <= nums.length <= 10^4`
- `-10^4 <= nums[i], target <= 10^4`
- `nums` is sorted ascending with distinct values

=== hints
- You are looking for the first index whose value is `>= target` (the "lower bound").
- Keep the invariant: the answer is always inside `[lo, hi]`.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int searchInsert(vector<int>& nums, int target) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::searchInsert);
    h.readonly();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        auto v = g.distinct(std::max(n, 1), -3 * n - 10, 3 * n + 10);
        std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
        int t = g.coin() ? g.pick(v) : g.irange(-3 * n - 12, 3 * n + 12);
        return lc::lines(v, t);
    });
}

=== approach
Binary search for the **lower bound**: the first index `i` with `nums[i] >= target`. Keep a half-open range `[lo, hi)` that always contains the answer, with `hi = n` meaning "insert at the end". If `nums[mid] < target`, the answer lies right of `mid`; otherwise it is `mid` or to its left. When `lo == hi`, that is the answer: either the target's index or its insertion point.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int searchInsert(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        int lo = 0, hi = nums.size();           // answer is in [lo, hi]
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;       // avoids overflow of lo + hi
            if (nums[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;   // answer is right of mid
            else hi = mid;                          // mid could be the answer
        }
        return lo;
    }
};

=== examples
[1,3,5,6]
5
> 2

[1,3,5,6]
2
> 1

[1,3,5,6]
7
> 4

=== tests
[1]
0
> 0

[1]
1
> 0

[1]
2
> 1

[-5,-2,0,4,9]
-3
> 1

@@@ problem 74 search-a-2d-matrix
title: Search a 2D Matrix
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Binary Search, Matrix
params: matrix, target
time: O(log(m · n))
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=logn space=1 unit=number_of_cells

=== description
You are given an `m x n` integer matrix where each row is sorted ascending and the first value of each row is greater than the last value of the previous row. Return `true` if `target` is in the matrix.

Your solution must run in **O(log(m · n))** time.

=== constraints
- `1 <= m, n <= 100`
- `-10^4 <= matrix[i][j], target <= 10^4`

=== hints
- Read row by row, the matrix is one sorted array of `m · n` elements.
- Index `k` of that virtual array is `matrix[k / n][k % n]`.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    bool searchMatrix(vector<vector<int>>& matrix, int target) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::searchMatrix);
    h.readonly();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int cells) {
        int r = std::max(1, (int)std::sqrt((double)cells) + g.irange(-2, 2)), c = std::max(1, cells / r);
        auto v = g.sorted_ints(r * c, -3LL * r * c - 10, 3LL * r * c + 10);
        std::vector<std::vector<int>> m(r);
        for (int i = 0; i < r; ++i) m[i].assign(v.begin() + i * c, v.begin() + (i + 1) * c);
        for (int i = 1; i < r; ++i) if (m[i][0] <= m[i - 1][c - 1]) for (int j = 0; j < c; ++j) m[i][j] += 0;
        // make rows strictly increasing across boundaries
        long long last = LLONG_MIN;
        for (auto &row : m) for (auto &x : row) { if (x <= last) x = (int)(last + 1); last = x; }
        int t = g.coin() ? m[g.irange(0, r - 1)][g.irange(0, c - 1)] : (int)g.range(-3LL * r * c - 12, last + 2);
        return lc::lines(m, t);
    });
}

=== approach
Because every row starts after the previous one ends, reading the matrix row by row gives one **sorted sequence** of `m · n` values. Binary search over indices `0 .. m·n - 1` of that virtual array, converting index `k` to `matrix[k / n][k % n]`. That is O(log(m · n)) with no extra memory.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    bool searchMatrix(vector<vector<int>>& matrix, int target) {
        int m = matrix.size(), n = matrix[0].size();
        int lo = 0, hi = m * n - 1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            int v = matrix[mid / n][mid % n];      // virtual 1-D index -> row, column
            if (v == target) return true;
            if (v < target) lo = mid + 1;
            else hi = mid - 1;
        }
        return false;
    }
};

=== examples
[[1,3,5,7],[10,11,16,20],[23,30,34,60]]
3
> true

[[1,3,5,7],[10,11,16,20],[23,30,34,60]]
13
> false

=== tests
[[1]]
1
> true

[[1]]
2
> false

[[1,3]]
3
> true

[[1],[3],[5]]
4
> false

@@@ problem 162 find-peak-element
title: Find Peak Element
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Binary Search
params: nums
time: O(log n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=logn space=1

=== description
A **peak** is an element strictly greater than its neighbours. Given `nums` with `nums[i] != nums[i + 1]` for all valid `i`, return the index of **any** peak. Imagine `nums[-1] = nums[n] = -∞`, so the ends count as higher than the outside.

The judge accepts any peak: it prints `"a peak index"` when yours is one.

Your algorithm must run in **O(log n)** time.

=== constraints
- `1 <= nums.length <= 1000`
- `-2^31 <= nums[i] <= 2^31 - 1`
- `nums[i] != nums[i + 1]`

=== hints
- Look at `nums[mid]` and `nums[mid + 1]`. If the sequence goes up there, is a peak guaranteed to the right?

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int findPeakElement(vector<int>& nums) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        auto nums = c.arg<std::vector<int>>();
        int r = -1;
        Solution s;
        c.time([&] { r = s.findPeakElement(nums); });
        int n = nums.size();
        auto at = [&](int i) { return (i < 0 || i >= n) ? LLONG_MIN : (long long)nums[i]; };
        if (r >= 0 && r < n && at(r) > at(r - 1) && at(r) > at(r + 1)) c.out_text("\"a peak index\"");
        else c.out(r);
    });
    h.readonly();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 1);
        std::vector<int> v(n);
        v[0] = g.irange(-1000, 1000);
        for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
            int step = g.irange(1, 5);
            v[i] = v[i - 1] + (g.coin(n > 40 ? 0.02 : 0.5) ? -step : step);   // benchmark: long rising runs
        }
        return lc::lines(v);
    });
}

=== approach
Binary search on the **slope**. Compare `nums[mid]` with `nums[mid + 1]`:

- if `nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1]`, the sequence rises to the right. Following it upward, it must eventually peak before the `-∞` wall at the end, so a peak exists in `[mid + 1, hi]`;
- otherwise a peak exists in `[lo, mid]` (possibly `mid` itself).

Each step halves the range, and when `lo == hi` that index is a peak.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int findPeakElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        int lo = 0, hi = (int)nums.size() - 1;
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1]) lo = mid + 1;   // climbing: peak on the right
            else hi = mid;                                  // descending: peak at mid or left
        }
        return lo;
    }
};

=== examples
[1,2,3,1]
> "a peak index"
: Index 2 (value 3) is the only peak.

[1,2,1,3,5,6,4]
> "a peak index"
: Both index 1 and index 5 are peaks.

=== tests
[1]
> "a peak index"

[1,2]
> "a peak index"

[2,1]
> "a peak index"

[-2147483648,2147483647,-2147483648]
> "a peak index"

@@@ problem 33 search-in-rotated-sorted-array
title: Search in Rotated Sorted Array
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Binary Search
params: nums, target
time: O(log n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=logn space=1

=== description
An ascending array of **distinct** integers was rotated at an unknown pivot, e.g. `[0,1,2,4,5,6,7]` became `[4,5,6,7,0,1,2]`. Given the rotated array `nums` and a `target`, return the index of `target`, or `-1` if it is not present.

Your algorithm must run in **O(log n)** time.

=== constraints
- `1 <= nums.length <= 5000`
- `-10^4 <= nums[i], target <= 10^4`
- all values are distinct; `nums` is a rotated ascending array

=== hints
- Split at `mid`: at least one of the two halves is sorted normally.
- Check whether the target lies inside the sorted half's range; if so search there, else search the other half.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int search(vector<int>& nums, int target) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::search);
    h.readonly();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 1);
        auto v = g.distinct(n, -3 * n - 10, 3 * n + 10);
        std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
        std::rotate(v.begin(), v.begin() + g.irange(0, n - 1), v.end());
        int t = g.coin() ? g.pick(v) : g.irange(-3 * n - 12, 3 * n + 12);
        return lc::lines(v, t);
    });
}

=== approach
Standard binary search, but at each step decide which half is **sorted**:

- if `nums[lo] <= nums[mid]`, the left half `[lo, mid]` is sorted. If `nums[lo] <= target < nums[mid]` the target can only be there; otherwise go right;
- else the right half `[mid, hi]` is sorted; test `nums[mid] < target <= nums[hi]` the same way.

Each step discards half the range: O(log n).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int search(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        int lo = 0, hi = (int)nums.size() - 1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (nums[mid] == target) return mid;
            if (nums[lo] <= nums[mid]) {                        // left half is sorted
                if (nums[lo] <= target && target < nums[mid]) hi = mid - 1;
                else lo = mid + 1;
            } else {                                            // right half is sorted
                if (nums[mid] < target && target <= nums[hi]) lo = mid + 1;
                else hi = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
};

=== examples
[4,5,6,7,0,1,2]
0
> 4

[4,5,6,7,0,1,2]
3
> -1

[1]
0
> -1

=== tests
[1]
1
> 0

[3,1]
1
> 1

[5,1,3]
5
> 0

[6,7,1,2,3,4,5]
7
> 1

@@@ problem 34 find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array
title: Find First and Last Position of Element in Sorted Array
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Binary Search
params: nums, target
time: O(log n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=logn space=1

=== description
Given `nums` sorted in non-decreasing order, find the first and last positions of `target`. Return `[-1, -1]` if it does not occur.

Your algorithm must run in **O(log n)** time (so scanning the whole run of equal values is not allowed).

=== constraints
- `0 <= nums.length <= 10^5`
- `-10^9 <= nums[i], target <= 10^9`
- `nums` is sorted in non-decreasing order

=== hints
- Two binary searches: the first index with value `>= target`, and the first index with value `> target`.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> searchRange(vector<int>& nums, int target) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::searchRange);
    h.readonly();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        auto v = g.sorted_ints(n, -n / 4 - 3, n / 4 + 3);
        int t = (!v.empty() && g.coin()) ? g.pick(v) : g.irange(-n / 4 - 5, n / 4 + 5);
        return lc::lines(v, t);
    });
}

=== approach
Write one binary search, `lowerBound(x)`: the first index with `nums[i] >= x`. Then

- `first = lowerBound(target)`; if it is past the end or `nums[first] != target`, the target is absent;
- `last = lowerBound(target + 1) - 1` (using `long long` to avoid overflow at `INT_MAX`).

Two O(log n) searches, no scan over the run of equal values.

=== solution
class Solution {
    // First index i with nums[i] >= x (nums.size() if none).
    int lowerBound(const vector<int> &nums, long long x) {
        int lo = 0, hi = nums.size();
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (nums[mid] < x) lo = mid + 1;
            else hi = mid;
        }
        return lo;
    }

public:
    vector<int> searchRange(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        int first = lowerBound(nums, target);
        if (first == (int)nums.size() || nums[first] != target) return {-1, -1};
        int last = lowerBound(nums, (long long)target + 1) - 1;   // end of the run
        return {first, last};
    }
};

=== examples
[5,7,7,8,8,10]
8
> [3,4]

[5,7,7,8,8,10]
6
> [-1,-1]

[]
0
> [-1,-1]

=== tests
[1]
1
> [0,0]

[2,2]
2
> [0,1]

[1,2,3]
3
> [2,2]

[2147483647,2147483647]
2147483647
> [0,1]

@@@ problem 153 find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array
title: Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Binary Search
params: nums
time: O(log n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=logn space=1

=== description
An ascending array of **unique** values was rotated between 1 and `n` times; e.g. `[0,1,2,4,5,6,7]` might become `[4,5,6,7,0,1,2]`. Return the **minimum** element.

Your algorithm must run in **O(log n)** time.

=== constraints
- `1 <= n <= 5000`
- `-5000 <= nums[i] <= 5000`, all unique

=== hints
- Compare `nums[mid]` with `nums[hi]`. If `nums[mid] > nums[hi]`, where must the minimum be?

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int findMin(vector<int>& nums) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::findMin);
    h.readonly();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 1);
        auto v = g.distinct(n, -3 * n - 10, 3 * n + 10);
        std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
        std::rotate(v.begin(), v.begin() + g.irange(0, n - 1), v.end());
        return lc::lines(v);
    });
}

=== approach
The minimum is the only point where the order "drops". Compare the middle element with the **last** element of the current range:

- if `nums[mid] > nums[hi]`, the drop (and the minimum) lies to the right of `mid`;
- otherwise `mid..hi` is sorted, so the minimum is `mid` or to its left.

Shrink until one element remains.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int findMin(vector<int>& nums) {
        int lo = 0, hi = (int)nums.size() - 1;
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (nums[mid] > nums[hi]) lo = mid + 1;   // the drop is to the right of mid
            else hi = mid;                            // mid..hi sorted: min at mid or left
        }
        return nums[lo];
    }
};

=== examples
[3,4,5,1,2]
> 1

[4,5,6,7,0,1,2]
> 0

[11,13,15,17]
> 11
: Rotated n times, so the array is back in sorted order.

=== tests
[1]
> 1

[2,1]
> 1

[1,2]
> 1

[5,1,2,3,4]
> 1

@@@ problem 4 median-of-two-sorted-arrays
title: Median of Two Sorted Arrays
difficulty: Hard
topics: Array, Binary Search, Divide and Conquer
params: nums1, nums2
time: O(log(min(m, n)))
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=logn space=1 unit=m_=_n

=== description
Given two sorted arrays `nums1` (size `m`) and `nums2` (size `n`), return the **median** of the combined data. Answers within `10^-5` are accepted.

The overall run time must be **O(log(m + n))**.

=== constraints
- `0 <= m, n <= 1000`, `1 <= m + n <= 2000`
- `-10^6 <= values <= 10^6`
- both arrays are sorted ascending

=== hints
- Merging is O(m + n): too slow for the requirement.
- Binary search a **cut** in the smaller array: `i` elements from `nums1` and `half - i` from `nums2` form the left half. The cut is right when both left maxima are `<=` both right minima.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    double findMedianSortedArrays(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::findMedianSortedArrays);
    h.readonly();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        int a = n <= 40 ? g.irange(0, n) : n, b = n <= 40 ? g.irange(a == 0 ? 1 : 0, std::max(1, n)) : n;
        return lc::lines(g.sorted_ints(a, -1000000, 1000000), g.sorted_ints(b, -1000000, 1000000));
    });
}

=== approach
Partition both arrays so that the left parts together hold `half = (m + n + 1) / 2` elements. Binary search `i` (elements taken from the smaller array `A`); then `j = half - i` elements come from `B`.

The partition is correct when `A[i-1] <= B[j]` and `B[j-1] <= A[i]` (with `±∞` beyond the ends). If `A[i-1] > B[j]`, too many elements come from `A`: move left; otherwise move right. At the correct partition the median is `max(left maxima)` for odd totals, or the average of `max(left maxima)` and `min(right minima)` for even totals.

Searching only the smaller array gives O(log(min(m, n))).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    double findMedianSortedArrays(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {
        vector<int> &A = nums1.size() <= nums2.size() ? nums1 : nums2;   // A = smaller array
        vector<int> &B = nums1.size() <= nums2.size() ? nums2 : nums1;
        int m = A.size(), n = B.size();
        int half = (m + n + 1) / 2;                   // size of the left half
        int lo = 0, hi = m;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int i = (lo + hi) / 2;                    // take i from A...
            int j = half - i;                         // ...and j from B
            long long aLeft  = i > 0 ? A[i - 1] : LLONG_MIN;
            long long aRight = i < m ? A[i]     : LLONG_MAX;
            long long bLeft  = j > 0 ? B[j - 1] : LLONG_MIN;
            long long bRight = j < n ? B[j]     : LLONG_MAX;
            if (aLeft > bRight) hi = i - 1;           // too many from A
            else if (bLeft > aRight) lo = i + 1;      // too few from A
            else {                                    // valid partition
                long long leftMax = max(aLeft, bLeft);
                if ((m + n) % 2 == 1) return leftMax;
                long long rightMin = min(aRight, bRight);
                return (leftMax + rightMin) / 2.0;
            }
        }
        return 0.0;   // unreachable for valid input
    }
};

=== examples
[1,3]
[2]
> 2.00000
: The merged array is [1,2,3].

[1,2]
[3,4]
> 2.50000
: The merged array is [1,2,3,4]: (2 + 3) / 2 = 2.5.

=== tests
[]
[1]
> 1.00000

[2]
[]
> 2.00000

[1,1]
[1,1]
> 1.00000

[1,5,9]
[2,3,4,10]
> 4.00000

[-1000000]
[1000000]
> 0.00000
